    "html": "<p><strong>Ramesh Aravind was born on 10 September 1964 in Kumbakonam, Tamil Nadu, and made his film debut in 1986.</strong> Over the following decades he has acted in more than 140 films, directed eight, hosted <em>Weekend With Ramesh</em> on Zee Kannada, written several books and become a sought-after motivational speaker based in Bengaluru.</p>\n<h2>Quick facts</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Name</td>\n<td>Dr Ramesh Aravind (Kannada: ರಮೇಶ್ ಅರವಿಂದ್)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Born</td>\n<td>10 September 1964, Kumbakonam, Tamil Nadu, India</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Based in</td>\n<td>Bengaluru, Karnataka</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Active since</td>\n<td>1986</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Occupations</td>\n<td>Actor, director, screenwriter, television host, author, motivational speaker, producer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Languages of work</td>\n<td>Kannada, Tamil, Telugu, Hindi, English</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Honorary doctorate</td>\n<td>Rani Channamma University, Belagavi (2022)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2>Early life</h2>\n<p>Ramesh Aravind was born in Kumbakonam in Tamil Nadu and built his career in Karnataka, where he is based today in Bengaluru. That background helps explain why he has always moved comfortably between Kannada and Tamil cinema and speaks to audiences in both languages.</p>\n<h2>Career timeline</h2>\n<p><strong>1986 – Film debut.</strong> He makes his debut in the Kannada film <em>Sundara Swapnagalu</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>1987–1988 – Entering Tamil and Telugu cinema.</strong> His Tamil debut, <em>Manathil Uruthi Vendum</em>, follows in 1987, and his Telugu debut, <em>Rudraveena</em>, in 1988.</p>\n<p><strong>1990s – Leading man of Kannada cinema.</strong> He stars in Tamil films such as <em>Duet</em> (1994) and <em>Sathi Leelavathi</em> (1995), and in a run of much-loved Kannada films, including <em>America America</em>, <em>Amrutha Varshini</em>, <em>O Mallige</em> and <em>Nammoora Mandara Hoove</em> (all 1997), and <em>Hoomale</em> (1998), which he also wrote.</p>\n<p><strong>1996–1998 – Major awards.</strong> He wins the Karnataka State Film Award for Best Actor for <em>America America</em>, the Filmfare Award South for Best Actor – Kannada for <em>Amrutha Varshini</em> (1997) and <em>Hoomale</em> (1998), and the Udaya Film Award for Best Actor for <em>O Mallige</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>2000s – A new role behind the camera.</strong> After films such as <em>Kotigalu Saar Kotigalu</em> (2001), the Tamil <em>Panchatanthiram</em> (2002) and <em>Apthamitra</em> (2004), he directs his first film, <em>Rama Shama Bhama</em> (2005). It wins him the Udaya Film Award for Best Debut Director in 2006. <em>Sathyavan Savithri</em> (2007), <em>Accident</em> (2008) and <em>Venkata in Sankata</em> (2009) follow.</p>\n<p><strong>2010s – Director and television host.</strong> He directs <em>Nammanna Don</em> (2012), the Tamil film <em>Uttama Villain</em> (2015) starring Kamal Haasan, and <em>Sundaranga Jaana</em> (2016), and acts in <em>Pushpaka Vimana</em> (2017). On television, <em>Weekend With Ramesh</em> on Zee Kannada makes him a household name across Karnataka. He receives Pride of Zee Kannada (2015) and the Nadaprabhu Kempegowda Award (2018).</p>\n<p><strong>2020s – Thrillers, a doctorate and new audiences.</strong> He stars as the detective in <em>Shivaji Surathkal</em> (2020) and <em>Shivaji Surathkal 2</em> (2023), and directs <em>100</em> (2021). In 2022 Rani Channamma University awards him an honorary doctorate. He judges <em>Mahanati</em> on Zee Kannada (2024), acts in <em>Bhairadevi</em> (2024), and wins the Chandanavana Film Critics Academy Award for Best Actor for <em>Shivaji Surathkal 2</em>. The Tamil edition of his book, <em>Anbudan Ramesh</em>, is published in 2024.</p>\n<h2>Beyond films</h2>\n<p>Alongside cinema, he has built a second career as an author and speaker. His Kannada book <em>Preetiyinda Ramesh</em> went through five editions in five months, and he speaks at companies, colleges and conferences in English, Kannada and Tamil. As a producer, his television serials include <em>Supernatural Fiction</em> and <em>Sundari</em> on Udaya TV and <em>Neenadena</em> and <em>Aase</em> on Star Suvarna.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>How old is Ramesh Aravind?</h3>\n<p>He was born on 10 September 1964, so his age can be worked out from that date.</p>\n<h3>Where was Ramesh Aravind born?</h3>\n<p>In Kumbakonam, Tamil Nadu, India.</p>\n<h3>When did Ramesh Aravind start acting?</h3>\n<p>His first film, the Kannada film <em>Sundara Swapnagalu</em>, was released in 1986.</p>\n<h2>Related pages</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/ramesh-aravind-biography\">Full biography</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/awards\">Awards and honours</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/director-portfolio\">Films as director</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Sources</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ramesh_Aravind\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ramesh_Aravind</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.ramesharavind.com\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">https://www.ramesharavind.com</a></li>\n</ul>\n"
