    "html": "<p><strong>Ramesh Aravind has directed eight feature films: Rama Shama Bhama (2005), Sathyavan Savithri (2007), Accident (2008), Venkata in Sankata (2009), Nammanna Don (2012), Uttama Villain (2015), Sundaranga Jaana (2016) and 100 (2021).</strong> Seven are in Kannada and one, Uttama Villain, is in Tamil. His first film as director won him the Udaya Film Award for Best Debut Director.</p>\n<h2>The complete list</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Film</th>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Language</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Rama Shama Bhama</td>\n<td>2005</td>\n<td>Kannada</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Sathyavan Savithri</td>\n<td>2007</td>\n<td>Kannada</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Accident</td>\n<td>2008</td>\n<td>Kannada</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Venkata in Sankata</td>\n<td>2009</td>\n<td>Kannada</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Nammanna Don</td>\n<td>2012</td>\n<td>Kannada</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Uttama Villain</td>\n<td>2015</td>\n<td>Tamil</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Sundaranga Jaana</td>\n<td>2016</td>\n<td>Kannada</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>100</td>\n<td>2021</td>\n<td>Kannada</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Across sixteen years, he has moved between family comedy, mystery, drama and thriller. In several of these films he worked in front of the camera as well as behind it, and on some he also shares the writing credit.</p>\n<h2>From actor to director</h2>\n<p>By the time he directed his first film, Ramesh Aravind had already spent almost two decades as an actor. He made his debut in the Kannada film <em>Sundara Swapnagalu</em> in 1986 and became one of the most familiar faces of 1990s Kannada cinema through films such as <em>America America</em>, <em>Amrutha Varshini</em>, <em>O Mallige</em> and <em>Hoomale</em>. Those years in front of the camera gave him a close view of how stories, performances and audiences work together, which shaped the kind of director he became.</p>\n<p>His first directorial film, <em>Rama Shama Bhama</em> (2005), was a comedy in which he also acted alongside Kamal Haasan and Urvashi. It won him the <strong>Udaya Film Award for Best Debut Director in 2006</strong>, and it also earned him the Suvarna Film Award for Best Actor in a comedy role. You can read more about it in our post on <em>Rama Shama Bhama</em>.</p>\n<h2>The range of his work as a director</h2>\n<p>Looking at the eight films together, a few threads stand out:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Comedy with heart.</strong> <em>Rama Shama Bhama</em> turns a married man&#39;s unexpected attraction during a house-hunting trip into a comedy of complications.</li>\n<li><strong>Mystery and drama.</strong> <em>Accident</em> (2008) follows a man trying to understand whether his activist wife&#39;s death was truly an accident. He co-wrote it with Rajendra Karanth and ShiJa, and acted in it alongside Aporup Acharya and Dinesh Babu.</li>\n<li><strong>Working across languages.</strong> <em>Uttama Villain</em> (2015) took him into Tamil cinema as a director, working with Kamal Haasan, K. Balachander and K. Viswanath on a story about a superstar&#39;s final wish to make a comedy with his mentor.</li>\n<li><strong>A thriller for the digital age.</strong> <em>100</em> (2021) is a cat-and-mouse story between a police officer and a cyberstalker, which he wrote, directed and starred in.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Directing and acting at the same time</h2>\n<p>In three of the eight films covered on this site (<em>Rama Shama Bhama</em>, <em>Accident</em> and <em>100</em>), Ramesh Aravind both directed and acted. That double role is uncommon, and it reflects how naturally the two jobs sit together in his career. He is still very active as an actor: recent films include <em>Shivaji Surathkal</em> (2020), <em>Shivaji Surathkal 2</em> (2023) and <em>Bhairadevi</em> (2024), all directed by others.</p>\n<h2>Where to see more</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/director-portfolio\">director page</a> on the official website shows posters and short synopses for his directorial work. The <a href=\"/movies\">movies page</a> lists his best-known films as an actor alongside the full list of films he has directed.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>How many films has Ramesh Aravind directed?</h3>\n<p>Ramesh Aravind has directed eight feature films, seven in Kannada and one in Tamil, between 2005 and 2021.</p>\n<h3>What was the first film Ramesh Aravind directed?</h3>\n<p>His first film as director was <em>Rama Shama Bhama</em> (2005), which won him the Udaya Film Award for Best Debut Director in 2006.</p>\n<h3>Has Ramesh Aravind directed a Tamil film?</h3>\n<p>Yes. He directed the Tamil film <em>Uttama Villain</em> (2015), starring Kamal Haasan.</p>\n<h2>Related pages</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/director-portfolio\">Film director – Dr Ramesh Aravind</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/movies\">Movies &amp; filmography</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/awards\">Awards &amp; honours</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/ramesh-aravind-biography\">Biography</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Sources</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ramesh_Aravind\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia – Ramesh Aravind</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.ramesharavind.com\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official website – www.ramesharavind.com</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.imdb.com/name/nm0712076/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">IMDb – Ramesh Aravind</a></li>\n</ul>\n"
