    "html": "<p><strong>Rama Shama Bhama (2005) was directed by Ramesh Aravind. It was his first film as a director.</strong> The Kannada comedy starred Kamal Haasan, Ramesh Aravind and Urvashi, and it won him the Udaya Film Award for Best Debut Director in 2006, opening a new chapter in a career that had begun in front of the camera in 1986.</p>\n<h2>What the film is about</h2>\n<p><em>Rama Shama Bhama</em> is a comedy built around an everyday situation that spirals. A married man&#39;s life takes an unexpected turn when he falls for another woman during a house-hunting trip, and what follows is a chain of comic complications as he tries to keep his two worlds apart.</p>\n<p>The premise is simple and relatable, which is part of its appeal: the humour comes from ordinary people caught in a situation that keeps getting harder to explain.</p>\n<h2>Cast and writers</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Role</th>\n<th>Name</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Director</td>\n<td>Ramesh Aravind</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Writers</td>\n<td>Ramesh Aravind, Rajendra Karanth, Balu Mahendra</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stars</td>\n<td>Kamal Haasan, Ramesh Aravind, Urvashi</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Language</td>\n<td>Kannada</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Year</td>\n<td>2005</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Ramesh Aravind took on three jobs on this film: director, co-writer and actor. Directing a film in which he also plays a lead role, opposite an actor of Kamal Haasan&#39;s stature, made it an ambitious first project.</p>\n<h2>Awards for Rama Shama Bhama</h2>\n<p>The film brought Ramesh Aravind recognition on both sides of the camera:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Udaya Film Awards (2006): Best Debut Director</strong>, for <em>Rama Shama Bhama</em></li>\n<li><strong>Suvarna Film Awards (2006): Best Actor (hero in a comedy role)</strong>, for <em>Rama Shama Bhama</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Winning a directing award and an acting award for the same film is a good measure of how well the film worked as a whole: the writing, direction and performances all landed with audiences and juries.</p>\n<h2>Why it matters in his career</h2>\n<p>By 2005, Ramesh Aravind was already well known as an actor. He had made his debut in <em>Sundara Swapnagalu</em> (1986), and in the 1990s he won the Karnataka State Film Award for Best Actor for <em>America America</em> and two Filmfare Awards South for <em>Amrutha Varshini</em> and <em>Hoomale</em>.</p>\n<p><em>Rama Shama Bhama</em> showed that he could also lead a film from behind the camera. It was the start of a directing career that now spans eight films, including <em>Accident</em> (2008), the Tamil film <em>Uttama Villain</em> (2015) and the thriller <em>100</em> (2021). His collaboration with Kamal Haasan on this film also carried forward: a decade later, he directed Kamal Haasan again in <em>Uttama Villain</em>.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>Who directed Rama Shama Bhama?</h3>\n<p>Ramesh Aravind directed <em>Rama Shama Bhama</em> (2005). It was his first film as a director.</p>\n<h3>Who acted in Rama Shama Bhama?</h3>\n<p>The film starred Kamal Haasan, Ramesh Aravind and Urvashi.</p>\n<h3>Did Rama Shama Bhama win any awards?</h3>\n<p>Yes. Ramesh Aravind won the Udaya Film Award for Best Debut Director (2006) and the Suvarna Film Award for Best Actor in a comedy role (2006) for the film.</p>\n<h2>Related pages</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/director-portfolio\">Film director – Dr Ramesh Aravind</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/awards\">Awards &amp; honours</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/movies\">Movies &amp; filmography</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Sources</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ramesh_Aravind\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia – Ramesh Aravind</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.ramesharavind.com\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official website – www.ramesharavind.com</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.imdb.com/name/nm0712076/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">IMDb – Ramesh Aravind</a></li>\n</ul>\n"
